IMF PortWatch
-------------

Datasets: strait_transits (the ships a day through the Strait of Hormuz,
by ship type, 1 January 2025 to 4 October 2026: 642 days), ports (the 68
ports PortWatch follows in the United Arab Emirates, Saudi Arabia, Qatar,
Bahrain, Kuwait, Iraq, Iran and Oman) and port_calls (each of those ports'
calls a day, by ship type, 1 January 2025 to 2 October 2026: 43,520 rows).

Publisher: the International Monetary Fund's PortWatch, on the satellite
AIS data of the UN Global Platform, which Kpler provides.
https://portwatch.imf.org

Licence: the IMF's copyright and usage terms, their "Special Terms for
Statistical Data": users "may download, extract, copy, create derivative
works, publish, distribute, and use Data obtained from IMF Sites", with
the IMF named as the source, any material transformation stated, and
"For any potential commercial reuse of IMF Data, email copyright@imf.org
to request permission." PortWatch's FAQ sends questions about commercial
redistribution of its datasets to the same address. Anyone who passes the
data on is asked to make reasonable efforts to have their users follow
these terms; whoever sells the data as a product of its own must say that
the IMF gives it free of charge.
https://www.imf.org/en/About/copyright-and-terms

Credit, as PortWatch's FAQ recommends for its port and chokepoint
activity: "Sources: Kpler; UN Global Platform; IMF PortWatch
(portwatch.imf.org)." In the form the IMF's terms give: Source:
International Monetary Fund, PortWatch, https://portwatch.imf.org.

Transformation, stated as the terms ask: the template keeps PortWatch's
daily counts unchanged and adds them up only on the map: the ships a day
over 1 March to 2 October in 2025 and in 2026, each port's change between
the two, and one row per counted transit (so the timeline counts ships).
Each port is placed on the sea of the nearest Natural Earth marine area;
the strait sits at PortWatch's own point for it. The counts rest on ships'
AIS transponders: PortWatch reports GPS jamming, AIS spoofing and ships
going dark at the strait in 2026, and possible anomalies on 27 and 28
April 2026.

