World Bank, Global Shipping Traffic Density
-------------------------------------------

Dataset: shipping_lanes (14,811 H3 cells at resolution 5, about 250 km²
each, between 33° and 65° E and 11° and 33° N)

Publisher: the World Bank, Development Data Hub, dataset 0037580, Global
Shipping Traffic Density; the grid of commercial ships' AIS positions,
January 2015 to February 2021, at 0.005°.
https://datacatalog.worldbank.org/search/dataset/0037580

Licence: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC-BY-4.0), as
the catalogue's record states.
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Credit, as the record's citation gives it: World Bank, Global Shipping
Traffic Density. Data source: IMF's World Seaborne Trade monitoring system
(Cerdeiro, Komaromi, Liu and Saeed, 2020).

Where and when: read on 2026-10-12 at 17:56:03 UTC; of the commercial
ships' archive

  https://datacatalogfiles.worldbank.org/ddh-published/0037580/5/DR0045405/shipdensity_commercial_.zip
  (480,353,369 bytes, last modified Thu, 27 Feb 2025 22:20:50 GMT)

only the grid's own entry was read, by range: ShipDensity_Commercial1.tif
(356,093,602 bytes deflated, SHA-256 87c1ddb30e9d9d0eacebdb4b9d5dd52679900cdf26cad78b70be26403d86343e;
9,818,136,931 bytes inflated, its CRC-32 checked against the archive's).

Kept, and changed: the positions inside the window, summed two pixels by
two and then into the H3 cell that holds each block's middle; each cell
with its count of positions. The map leaves out the cells with fewer than
ten million positions in six years.


